  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM EDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nEarly this afternoon GOES water vapor imagery showed strong \nsubsidence in the mid-levels over the U.P. as a shortwave was \npassing through Ontario. This was helping to keep the area \nprecipitation free. Daytime heating was resulting in scattered fair \nweather cumulus over the western half of the U.P. Over the east and \nclose to Lake Superior temperatures were cooler, limiting \ninstability and keeping the skies cloud free. Surface observations \nshowed temperatures were mainly in the 60s and winds remained breezy \nfrom the northwest over the east, especially near Lake Superior.\n\nOverall the area will remain under west to northwest flow aloft for \nthe next several days with upper level ridging centered over the \nwest-central part of the U.S. This will keep a stronger upper level \njet over the Upper Great Lakes along with precipitation chances as \nshortwaves round the ridge. Ensembles show the ridge amplifying over \nthe western U.S. next week and drier air moving south out of \nOntario, bringing limited precipitation chances for midweek.\n\nTonight the U.P. will remain between high pressure moving through \nthe midwest and a deep low over northern Quebec. This will help turn \nwinds southwesterly, bringing warmer air back into the region. Thus \nexpect lows to be warmer than last night, mainly upper 50s to mid \n60s. Highs tomorrow will reach into the upper 70s to low 80s, fairly \nclose to average. Some guidance is holding on to higher dewpoints \nand resultant daytime instability which could trigger a few \nafternoon showers over the east. Maintained the slight PoPs from the \nNBM that account for this.\n\nFriday will be a bit warmer yet with temperatures breaking the 80 \ndegree mark for most locations and as high as the mid 80s in the \nsoutherly winds downslope areas along Lake Superior. No \nprecipitation is anticipated during the day but late Friday night \nthe chance for showers and thunderstorms will increase over the \nKeweenaw and western U.P. as warm air and moisture advection \nincreases ahead of an approaching shortwave. These showers/storms \nwill likely weaken and diminish in coverage as they move through the \ncentral and east as they loose support from the low level jet. \nAnother round of showers and thunderstorms is expected during the \nafternoon and evening as a cold front pushes through the area. \nModest instability and shear look sufficient for a few strong to \nsevere thunderstorms. Please keep an eye on future forecasts as the \nthreat will likely evolve (higher or lower) as we get closer in time.\n\nAdditional shower and thunderstorm chances are expected for Sunday \ninto the early part of next week as additional shortwaves move \nthrough the area. Temperatures will be mainly in the 70s to mid 80s, \nwarmest west.",
